        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>TIER-2 AUDIT INTEGRATION (γ.4.8.F) — WRIGHT&#x27;S PREFACE EXPLICITLY DISTINGUISHES GEʽEZ MEQABYAN FROM VULGATE-TRANSLATION MACCABEES. Per TIER2_AUDIT.md §2 Finding 3(b), William Wright&#x27;s Preface to the 1877 *Catalogue* (p. v) states plainly: *&#x27;The Ethiopic Book of the Maccabees is also here in several copies; but the later translation of first and second Maccabees from the Latin text is rather curious than valuable.&#x27;* Wright thus **explicitly distinguishes** the Geʽez Meqabyan (መቃብያን) — the EOTC-canonical Tewahedo-distinctive text — from a separate, later Geʽez translation of the Latin (Vulgate) 1-2 Maccabees. Several British Museum volumes preserve BOTH texts side by side: entry XV (Or. 491) holds Geʽez Meqabyan (f. 118b, item 7) AND the Vulgate-translation 1st + 2nd Maccabees (f. 168a + f. 197b, items 8-9); entry XXVIII (Or. 504) holds Geʽez Meqabyan (f. 105b, item 5) AND the Vulgate-translation two Books of Maccabees (f. 82a + f. 95a, item 4); entry XXXI (Or. 505) holds Geʽez Meqabyan (f. 39b, item 2) AND the Vulgate-translation Maccabees (item 1). This is the **single most important framing-distinction in the entire Meqabyan project** — independently corroborated by Wright&#x27;s 1877 Preface. Distinct from LXX 1-4 Maccabees AND from the later Geʽez Vulgate-Maccabees translation: the Tewahedo-canonical Mäqabyan trilogy stands as its own distinct biblical text-family.</p></aside>',
