        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>IDOLS-AND-SORCERERS PURGE of Maqabis-of-Moab&#x27;s household (vv. 5-9; expands seed 3:16 + 3:17-19): removal of household-idols + expulsion of sorcerers + diviners + Torah-instruction from the captive Jewish children. The household-religious-reform pattern parallels: 2 KINGS 23:1-25 (Josiah&#x27;s reform — destroying high-places + groves + altars + pulling-down houses-of-male-prostitutes); 2 CHRONICLES 14:3-5 (Asa&#x27;s reform); 2 CHRONICLES 17:3-6 (Jehoshaphat&#x27;s reform); 2 KINGS 18:4 (Hezekiah breaking the bronze serpent + cutting-down groves); MATTHEW 12:43-45 par. (the cleansed-and-empty-house parable). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive contribution: the reform is GENTILE-LED (Maqabis-of-Moab is a Moabite-king who initiates Mosaic-religious-reform within his own pagan-household) — the most-explicit-Gentile-king-as-religious-reformer narrative in the EOTC canon. The chapter develops the converted-king as DOMESTIC reformer before re-emerging as deliverer-judge in Ch 6.</p></aside>',
