        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 5: MAQABIS-OF-MOAB INSTRUCTS HIS HOUSEHOLD IN TORAH from the Jewish captive-children he had brought from Jerusalem — the domestic-catechumenate INVERSION moment. The Gentile-king who had destroyed Jerusalem and enslaved its children now sits as PUPIL before those same captive-children, learning the Law-of-Moses from them. The reversal parallels: 2 KINGS 5:1-15 (Naaman the Syrian captain instructed-toward-faith by the captive-Israelite slave-girl); 1 KINGS 17:8-24 (Elijah hosted by the Zarephath widow — Phoenician-Gentile hospitality); 2 KINGS 6:8-23 (Elisha and the blinded-Syrian-soldiers fed-and-released as Aramean-Gentile evangelism); MATTHEW 8:5-13 (the centurion&#x27;s faith exceeds-Israel&#x27;s). The captive-child-as-teacher-of-king topos is one of the most structurally-developed inversions in the entire Meqabyan trilogy — anticipating Jesus&#x27;s &#x27;a little child shall lead them&#x27; (Isaiah 11:6 / read Christologically) and Matthew 18:3 (&#x27;except ye be converted, and become as little children&#x27;).</p></aside>',
