        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 7: SONS&#x27; REFUSAL-SPEECH addressed-to-Ṣiruṣaydan paralleling the 1 Mq 2:5-19 brothers&#x27; creation-confession-and-anti-idolatry-creed. The five-sons-of-Maqabis-of-Moab (named at 13:1) deliver an interlocking speech-cycle: each son speaks in turn refusing idol-sacrifice on creational + covenantal + moral + eschatological + Christological-or-messianic grounds (the five-fold-argumentation schema). The pattern parallels: 2 MACCABEES 7:1-42 (the seven brothers and their interlocking speeches — the Greek-Maccabean canonical-model for serial-martyr-speech-cycles); 4 MACCABEES 9-12 (the same seven brothers&#x27; speeches elaborated philosophically); DANIEL 3:16-18 (Shadrach + Meshach + Abednego&#x27;s &#x27;we will not serve thy gods&#x27; speech). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive feature: the FIVE-FOLD (not seven-fold) brothers-speech-cycle replicates the 1 Mq five-brothers structural number-symmetry (vs LXX 2 Macc seven-brothers). The Meqabyan compositional-signature of five-fold-martyr-structures across both 1 Mq and 2 Mq is one of Horovitz 1905&#x27;s clearest internal-coherence evidences.</p></aside>',
        "Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan I-III (Three Books of Meqabyan, መጽሐፈ መቃብያን) — Tewahedo broader-canon Ethiopian Maccabees, distinct from the Greek LXX 1-4 Maccabees (different content; shared title only). English translation from the Modern Amharic of the EOTC Bible (nehemiah-osc.org) by Claude (Anthropic) with collaborator, May 2026. Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication (archive.org/details/three-books-of-meqabyan-cc0-translation). Apparatus integrates Josef Horovitz, 'Das äthiopische Maccabäerbuch,' Zeitschrift für Assyriologie XIX (1905), pp. 194-233 — PD primary scholarly study. 64-citation third-pass audit verdict matrix; 57 verified, 4 errors corrected, 3 interpretive readings flagged, 7 newly discovered parallels added. Dating widened to Aksumite-to-Solomonic Ethiopian Christian range (4th-14th c. CE); precise composition date undetermined in current scholarship per Horovitz 1905 non-commitment. Three books: 1 Mq 36 chapters (Maqabis-of-Benjamin martyrology vs Ṣiruṣaydan); 2 Mq 21 chapters (Maqabis-of-Moab conversion + sons' martyrdom + Ṣiruṣaydan's death); 3 Mq 10 chapters (homiletic + angelological dialogue + Satan-refused-Adam tradition + resurrection-doctrine).",
