        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>OPENS Ch 10: POST-MORTEM CRIES-FOR-VINDICATION (vv. 1-5) — the martyrs in heaven cry-out for divine-vengeance-and-vindication. Direct parallel to REVELATION 6:9-11 (&#x27;I saw under the altar the souls of them that were slain for the word of God, and for the testimony which they held: and they cried with a loud voice, saying, How long, O Lord, holy and true, dost thou not judge and avenge our blood on them that dwell on the earth?&#x27;). Additional parallels: PSALM 13:1-2 + 89:46 (HOW-LONG laments); LUKE 18:1-8 (the persistent-widow parable as cry-for-vindication); GENESIS 4:10 (Abel&#x27;s blood crying-from-the-ground); HEBREWS 12:24 (&#x27;the blood of sprinkling, that speaketh better things than that of Abel&#x27;). The verse functions as the BRIDGE between completed-martyrdom (Ch 9) and the impending divine-vengeance-on-Ṣiruṣaydan (Ch 12). Tewahedo eschatology preserves the martyrs-cry-for-vindication motif as integral to the doctrine of just-judgment — souls-in-heaven actively-participate in petitioning for the final-judgment.</p></aside>',
