        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>PENITENTIAL-PSALM CITATION at Maqabis-of-Moab&#x27;s bereavement-prayer (vv. 9-15) — likely echoing PSALM 51 (the prototype-penitential-psalm — David after Bathsheba). The psalm&#x27;s structure: confession-of-sin + plea-for-mercy + restoration-request + commitment-to-praise. The standard EOTC penitential-psalms (Psalms 6, 32, 38, 51, 102, 130, 143 — the seven penitential-psalms of the Christian tradition) are integrated into Tewahedo liturgy at Säʿatat (Liturgy of the Hours), and Athanasius&#x27;s LETTER TO MARCELLINUS (γ.4.9.D ATTR_MARC source) is the canonical-patristic guidebook for penitential-psalm-praying-by-disposition. Meqabyan&#x27;s citation here makes 2 Mq an exegetical-bridge between LXX-Maccabean-prayer-tradition (1 Macc 4:30-33 + 2 Macc 1:24-29 — the Hasmonean prayers) and the Davidic-penitential-psalm-tradition. The bereaved-king&#x27;s prayer locates him in the canonical-prayer-tradition rather than as a tragic-mourning figure.</p></aside>',
