        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>SONS-OF-MAQABIS-OF-MOAB CONFRONT ṢIRUṢAYDAN — pre-martyrdom confrontation-speech (vv. 5-9; expands seed 12:11 villain-arc context). The sons&#x27; confrontation parallels the standard PROPHET-CONFRONTS-KING pattern (cf. seed 2:1 Re&#x27;ay-confronts-Maqabis) but with martyrs-confronting-tyrant: DANIEL 5 (Daniel confronts Belshazzar — &#x27;thou art weighed in the balances, and art found wanting&#x27;); 2 KINGS 1:9-15 (Elijah confronts Ahaziah&#x27;s captains); JEREMIAH 38:14-28 (Jeremiah confronts Zedekiah in prison); MATTHEW 14:1-12 (John-the-Baptist confronts Herod-Antipas — and is beheaded). Meqabyan&#x27;s distinctive feature: the confrontation-speech-from-martyr-to-tyrant locates the martyrs in the PROPHETIC-INSTRUCTION genre rather than the passive-suffering-victim genre. The sons function as Daniel-like-prophets in their martyrdom — an aspect that links the 1 Mq + 2 Mq twin-martyrdom-cycles to the Danielic-confrontation tradition.</p></aside>',
