        '<aside class="note-comm-ethiopian"><strong>Meqabyan (Ethiopian tradition)</strong> <em>Second Book of Meqabyan (Mäṣḥafä Mäqabyan II)</em> <small>(c. 600 AD)</small><p>**CHRIST-ALLUSION DEBATED** per Horovitz 1905 &#x27;von Christus nirgends die Rede&#x27; caveat (vv. 10-14): the chapter contains Christological-readable allusions (&#x27;the one who shall come from the throne of God to judge the living and the dead&#x27; — Apostles&#x27; Creed-resonant). Horovitz 1905 p. 196 explicitly noted that MEQABYAN NOWHERE EXPLICITLY NAMES CHRIST (&#x27;von Christus nirgends die Rede ist&#x27;) — interpreting the text as deliberately OT-framed-and-pre-Christological. Tewahedo Christian reception reads passages like 2 Mq 19:10 as MESSIANIC-THROUGH-CHRISTIAN-OVERLAY: the &#x27;one-who-shall-come&#x27; is recognized-as-Christ by Christian-readers but is doctrinally-unspecified in the text-as-written. Per CROSS_REFERENCE_APPENDIX §62: this verse is one of THREE INTERPRETIVE-FLAGGED MEQABYAN PASSAGES (Tier 3 reframing per SOURCES.md §7) — the Christological reading is fully-compatible with the text but not text-determined. The verse functions as a CHRISTIAN-READER-RECEPTION-FOCUS: where Christian-readers naturally see Christ-prophecy, while non-Christological readings remain textually-defensible (the eschatological-Judge could be God-Himself or an angelic-emissary in pre-Christian-Jewish literature — cf. Daniel 7:13-14).</p></aside>',
